# Looking at files
> AmigaDOS **Type** shows a file and **More** pages it. In vsh `cat` shows a whole file,
> `head` its first lines, `tail` its last lines, and `less` pages it (Q leaves it).
$ printf 'one\ntwo\nthree\nfour\n' > notes.txt
? Show the whole file notes.txt (AmigaDOS: Type).
= grep -q three "$LEARN_OUT"
~ Type cat notes.txt.
! cat notes.txt
? Show only the first two lines of notes.txt.
= [ "$(wc -l < "$LEARN_OUT" | tr -d ' ')" = 2 ]
= grep -q one "$LEARN_OUT"
~ head takes -n and a number: head -n 2 notes.txt.
! head -n 2 notes.txt
? Show only the last line of notes.txt.
= [ "$(cat "$LEARN_OUT")" = four ]
~ tail -n 1 notes.txt.
! tail -n 1 notes.txt
> In vsh, `type` is not the AmigaDOS command. It is a builtin that tells you what a name is
> (as in bash), and `C:Type` stays the AmigaDOS command.
? Ask what cd is, with type.
= case "$LEARN_LINE" in "type cd") true ;; *) false ;; esac
~ Type type cd.
! type cd
